00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.078
No politics, no anything.

00:00:06.078 --> 00:00:08.340
(intense music)

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.840
- You've got your perspective.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.079
I just want to be happy,
don't you want to be happy?

00:00:15.079 --> 00:00:17.579
(jazzy music)

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.240
- Our first guest is Tom DeSanto.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.000
He's an extremely popular
writer and producer

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.440
known for some of the
biggest movie blockbusters,

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.030
including "X-Men" and "Transformers."

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.770
Tom will tell us more
about how he is partnering

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:35.930
with the CDC to challenge
kids to wear a mask

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.460
as they go back to school

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.008
through the "Heroes Wear Masks Challenge."

00:00:40.008 --> 00:00:41.090
And interestingly,

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.660
according to our VaynerMedia
back to school study,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.220
this will hopefully help not
only kids, but their moms,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.540
who we learned from our study,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.140
are two times more fearful of their kids

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.880
going back to school, then their dads are.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.049
Welcome, Tom.

00:00:55.049 --> 00:00:57.310
- Thank you all very much.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:00.440
I feel like we are forming
a real life Justice League

00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:02.662
to save (laughing)

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:05.170
Instead of pretend we're
doing it for for real.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.040
And hopefully we'll keep
some kids from getting sick

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.360
and bringing that home.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.640
And that's what we're all here for.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.090
- So Tom, before we go into
the back to school stuff,

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:13.923
just for the audience,

00:01:13.923 --> 00:01:16.200
'cause I know a lot of individuals

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.880
will be familiar with what
you've been involved in,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.210
but maybe not necessarily you.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.445
So, just a little back to
comic book fame, actually.

00:01:22.445 --> 00:01:24.563
What about a little three to four minutes

00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:27.500
on comic book number one, origin story.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.890
Like what kind of kid were you?

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.970
Like going over there?

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.225
Just professionally.

00:01:31.225 --> 00:01:34.032
'Cause I know a big percentage
of the audience here

00:01:34.032 --> 00:01:36.200
from 18 to 81 years old,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.067
have aspirations to do
things similar to you.

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.180
And I think we should spend
three, four minutes on that

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.560
and then we'll get into
some of the other stuff.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.460
- Got it.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.820
You know, as Gary was a baseball card kid,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.510
like my brother Dan, I
was a comic book kid.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.640
So, first story I have

00:01:53.002 --> 00:01:56.122
is we were across the street
at this little candy store

00:01:56.122 --> 00:01:57.790
in Elizabeth, New Jersey.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.180
And I was three years
old and my mom tells me

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.110
I threw myself on the ground

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.240
until she would buy me a flash comic book.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.320
I couldn't even read,

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.010
but I think I saw the
images of the superheroes

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.523
and I knew that was my
inner spirit animal.

00:02:13.772 --> 00:02:17.070
And then from there, you
know, I was, comic books,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.520
we lived in a four room apartment.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.630
We were five people.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.073
My dad was a cop in New Jersey.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.650
We never wanted for money or anything,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.303
but we weren't wealthy.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.440
And comic books allowed
me, in my imagination,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.200
to leave that four room apartment

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.440
and to go to different universes

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.540
and to go save the planet.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.060
And when you're a little kid,

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.220
I think one of the things
you really want to do

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.420
is make a difference on the planet.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.910
But you're small and you
don't have that strength

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.320
or that ability and superheroes give you

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.783
that aspirational thing.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.490
And look, if we were
back in ancient Greece,

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.670
we'd be teaching our kids
about Hercules arrogance

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.000
and his hubris.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.267
And we would be telling them,

00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:05.270
"Oh, we need to do these labors
in order to learn humility."

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.600
And that's what comic books
are when they're done right.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.083
These are our modern myths.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.390
And then that's why I think it spoke to me

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.780
in that Joseph Campbell 101 way.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.650
And if anyone doesn't
know Joseph Campbell,

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.530
if you really want to
understand the human ability

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.951
and storytelling,

00:03:20.951 --> 00:03:24.770
on why humanity was always
asking the same questions

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.980
around the planet, through their myths,

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.500
even though they had no
interconnection with each other,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.980
it's a great way to
take that college class,

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.280
on your own and go look
up those interviews

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.730
with Bill Moyers.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:39.960
It will give you a massive
insight into the global audience,

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.310
and what's in people's hearts.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.819
So for me as a comic book kid,

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:48.990
I remember one day, I was
also that kid rushing home

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:53.510
from football practice to
play "Dungeons and Dragons."

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:54.343
- Yes.

00:03:54.343 --> 00:03:55.176
You nerded out, Tom.

00:03:55.176 --> 00:03:56.910
Let's get everybody to understand,

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.290
you are nerding out.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.420
- I was nerding out.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.170
And I'm living proof
that your action figures

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:03.469
can become real.

00:04:03.469 --> 00:04:04.302
(laughing)

00:04:04.302 --> 00:04:06.690
And pay your mortgage many times over.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:07.850
And that's, for me,

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.029
that's the true success in life.

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:13.579
The one thing I would
say is don't chase money.

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:17.013
Money, if that is your only goal,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:18.970
you're always going to want more

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.020
and you're never going to be happy.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.260
Don't chase success.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.280
Success, you're always going to want more,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.113
and it's the same thing.

00:04:24.113 --> 00:04:25.490
But when you do what you love

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.290
and you can find success

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.490
and find financial freedom in that,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.410
that's where you're truly
a successful person.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.100
- Because you're enjoying it

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.600
instead of what it can bring to you.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.800
- Yes, yes.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.633
- I agree, I agree.

00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:42.160
- And that thing, your 80 year old self

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.753
is going to be very happy
if you live that life.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:46.690
